{
  "configuration_id": "config_014",
  "reason": "Evidence: config_033 (multimodal, pca_n2000, resnet50, avgpool, concat) is the current best at 0.6186 acc / 0.6103 F1, beating RNA-only (0.5907) and showing that even weak image features add value via concat fusion. All four completed experiments use avgpool aggregation, so the effect of transformer aggregation on multiprofile data is completely untested. Question: Does replacing avgpool with transformer aggregation improve performance by better modeling inter-profile dependencies, holding all other design choices (pca_n2000, resnet50, concat) constant? Why this is the best next step: It is a clean single-variable comparison against the current best configuration, isolating the aggregation design choice. Aggregation is a fundamental architectural decision that affects how multiprofile information is combined and could yield a larger gain than swapping the already-weak image encoder (resnet50 vs ViT-L showed only ~0.4% difference in image-only runs). Under a limited budget, resolving whether transformer aggregation matters before investing in more complex RNA encoders or fusion strategies is the highest-information next experiment."
}

